# What the lockfile produces rather than what it decides. `package-lock.json` is
# committed and `npm ci` rebuilds this from it, so a copy in the tree would only
# ever be a stale one — and it is a large one, which the Python tooling would then
# have to walk on its way over the repository.
node_modules

# Build output. `npm run build` writes it and nothing reads it back out of the
# tree; a deployment builds its own.
dist

# Vite's local overrides, which are one machine's and not this project's.
*.local
*.log

# Where the reference agents the browser walkthrough attacks are listening. Written
# by frontend/e2e/harness.py once they are up and deleted when it stops: an ephemeral
# port is a fact about one running process and never about this project.
e2e/served.json

# Playwright's own output. The report and the traces are one run's artefacts, and a
# failed run's trace is read where it was produced.
test-results
playwright-report

# The public half of the key pair the walkthrough signs with, written by
# frontend/e2e/harness.py for one run and deleted when it stops. The committed key is
# keys/agentaudit-signing.pub and this is never that: a per-run key belongs to one
# process, and a private half is written nowhere at all.
e2e/dev-signing.pub

# What `vercel link` wrote to remember which project this directory deploys to: the
# organisation id and the project id. Written by the CLI, not by hand, and it names
# one person's Vercel account rather than anything about this app — a clone should
# link to its own project rather than inherit mine. `vercel.json` beside it is the
# opposite and is committed: the rewrites are how the console reaches the bench, and
# a deployment that lost them would serve a console that calls nothing.
.vercel
